# 130x130 matrix multiplication over λ□ primitive 63-bit ints
# (ExampleMatMul.matMulProgram in the Arend project).
#
# Arend-authored: prog.ast is `ExamplePrint:matMulSexpr` and the two attribute
# files are `matMulAttrsCText`/`matMulAttrsOCamlText`, all regenerated by
# test/tools/regen-arend-asts.sh. prog.ast is byte-identical to the file the
# retired `matmul-ast` and `matmul-bench` cases checked in, so those two
# duplicates are gone: every backend of this program now provably reads the same
# bytes, which is what made `matmul-bench` a fair comparison in the first place.
#
# The C and OCaml backends need per-backend attribute files realizing the four
# primitive-op axioms: for C on the CertiRocq runtime's
# prim_int63_{add,mul,sub,eqb} (prog-c.attr), for OCaml on the hand-written
# Prim_int63 wrapper module (prog.attr). See ../../../runtime/int63/README.md.
#
# All three backends agree here only because 130^3 stays far below 2^62: C and
# OCaml implement genuine int63 (wrapping mod 2^63), the Java backend a 64-bit
# `long`. This is the one program in the corpus where that gap is observable,
# which is the whole reason the `c` backend is kept.
#
# The program to run while working (with ../lean-matmul-peano/): it has an
# upstream value, it is the fastest one that computes a real number, and it
# declares three backends -- so one invocation exercises both oracles at once.
# It is also the program the `Rt.curry` bug was found on, by exactly that
# backend differential.
expected=2197000
backends=java ocaml c
runtime=int63
xfail=
note=130^3; the corpus' only int63-vs-long witness, backends agree well below 2^62
